extends layout.pug

block content
    h1 Embedding #[span.text-brand Metabase] in Node

    p.
        This app shows a Metabase dashboard as a
        #[a(href="https://www.metabase.com/docs/latest/embedding/guest-embedding") guest embed]:
        the page loads Metabase's #[code embed.js] and renders a
        #[code &lt;metabase-dashboard&gt;] component with a token that this app signs on the server.
        People who view the dashboard don't need a Metabase account.

    h2 Each customer sees only its own data

    p.
        The dashboard has a #[strong Customer ID] filter that's set to #[strong Locked] in Metabase.
        When someone opens the dashboard, this app looks up the customer that the signed-in user belongs to and
        signs that customer's ID into the token. Metabase applies the filter before any data reaches the browser,
        and it rejects a value that doesn't come from a signed token.

    p Sign in as different people to see it work:

    table
        tr
            th Username
            th Password
            th Customer
            th Locked Customer ID
        tr
            td: code alice
            td: code password
            td Acme Corp
            td 5
        tr
            td: code bob
            td: code password
            td Acme Corp
            td 5
        tr
            td: code carol
            td: code password
            td Bluebird Labs
            td 12

    p.
        Alice and Bob work for the same customer, so they see the same data. Carol works for another customer,
        so she sees different data.

    p: a(href="/analytics") View the embedded dashboard

    h2 Public embeds

    p.
        For comparison, here's a #[a(href="https://www.metabase.com/docs/latest/embedding/public-links#public-embeds") public embed]:
        a public link to a dashboard in an iframe. A public embed doesn't use a token, so you can see it without signing in,
        and so can anyone else with the link. Filters in a public link's URL don't restrict anything, because anyone can
        change them. Use public embeds for data that everyone can see, and guest embeds when you need to restrict the data.

    // These public links come from the demo Metabase in this repository.
    iframe(src=`${metabaseSiteUrl}/public/dashboard/e104be98-2ea1-4dce-abff-aa3cfeddfe40` width="800" height="600" title="A public dashboard")

    p You can embed a single question the same way:

    iframe(src=`${metabaseSiteUrl}/public/question/938a446c-da62-479a-a1bd-913cd29a77ef` width="800" height="400" title="A public question")
